  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">HYDROLOGICAL REHABILITATION OF THE HUIZA</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Huizache-Caimanero Wetland in Sinaloa is used as a passage and wintering site for more than 200 species of waterfowl, shorebirds, colonial and terrestrial birds. Hurricane Willa in 2018 and an increase in shrimp farming and agricultural activities have generated progressive siltation, which has affected the hydrodynamics of the lagoon and triggered a cascade of changes, such as lower water quality, less water flow, increased salinity, loss of habitat for waterfowl, and reduced fisheries productivity. This project will carry out various activities. 1) It will clear the sediment from a network of channels, which will enhance 4,371.9 acres of wetland. It will use that sediment to build an island complex to improve habitat for ducks and shorebirds, and collect and propagate four mangrove species. Through these activities, it will also promote positive attitudes towards wetlands and birds among 1,000 youth. 2) It will prepare a participatory strategic plan and then protect that area of 4,371.9 acres through a 15-year &apos;Sustainable Use Unit&apos;. 3) It will also monitor salinity, temperature, and flood patterns of the restored ecosystem; and survey migratory birds, mangroves and fisheries. The results will be presented to local stakeholders and shared with global information reference sources .</narrative>
  </description>
